tests: prove fairness backlog for every small-request interval

Issue #4 fairness measurement closure.

Capacity, target generations and cumulative admissions now come from one
complete strictly parsed listcs reply. A nonzero compile-server port identifies
an actual worker row; pure submitters contribute no physical capacity even while
they own a large queued request backlog. Worker capacity is conservatively
bounded by max(current,maxJobs), so a lowered advertised ceiling cannot make the
proof easier.

Each of the ten small requests is proven individually. With monotone admission
and reply counters, admitted_before - replies_after is a lower bound throughout
the request interval; requiring it above the shared farm-capacity bound excludes
the endpoint-rebound gap. Topology, worker endpoints and target connection
generations must remain stable.

A pure arithmetic fixture retains the endpoint-rebound counterexample and checks
worker-role capacity classification. The historical endpoint-only mutant must
fail at its named trace. The integration gate reports maximum reply budgets and
minimum proven margins.
